roduction","url":"https://courses.ems.psu.edu/egee439/node/684","note":"5.5 mol methanol per mol oil fed, most of the excess is recycled.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":0,"pass":true}],"assumptions":["Oil is refined and degummed with free fatty acids below 0.5%, so no acid esterification step is needed. The oil is shown as triolein and the esters as methyl oleate.","Triolein heat of formation from the NIST WebBook (Freedman, Bagby et al., 1989, -2193.7 kJ/mol).","99.5% of the oil is converted over two reactors; the rest stays in the product as bound glycerides.","Sodium hydroxide catalyst at 0.5 wt% of oil leaves in the crude glycerol; acid neutralisation, soap splitting and glycerol refining are not modelled. Catalyst cost is not itemised.","60% of the excess methanol settles with the glycerol; 99.5% of all excess methanol is recovered and recycled.","Wash water 10% of ester flow, sent to treatment with the dryer condensate.","Steam bought from the site utility at the regional steam price."],"notes":[]}